  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Execution / Phishing-driven launcher", "technique": "T1059.001 + T1566.001", "what_happened": "At 2028-01-12 00:58:11 CET, Sysmon Event 1 fired on WS-MARKETING-21 (10.30.6.92): powershell.exe -nop -enc <base64> spawned with parentImage=outlook.exe, user=CORP\\john.doe. The base64-decoded command reveals 'Invoke-Kerberoast -OutputFormat Hashcat' — the PowerView function that automates Kerberos service-ticket harvesting. Parent=outlook.exe is the classic Office-attachment-to-PowerShell entry vector: john.doe opened a malicious attachment (likely .docm with macro or .lnk) which spawned an obfuscated PowerShell launcher.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=powershell.exe + commandLine contains '-nop -enc' + parentImage=outlook.exe + user=CORP\\john.doe. The -enc (EncodedCommand) flag is the Base64 obfuscation marker; the Invoke-Kerberoast call in the decoded blob is the tooling fingerprint.", "first_seen": "2028-01-12T00:58:11+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Credential Access (Kerberoasting)", "technique": "T1558.003", "what_happened": "Between 01:09:44 and 01:11:13 CET (1m 29s), DC-CORP-01 (10.30.2.10) logged SIX Windows Security Event 4769 'A Kerberos service ticket was requested' from john.doe (TargetUserName=john.doe@CORP.LOCAL, IpAddress=10.30.6.92). Each request carries TicketEncryptionType=0x17 (RC4-HMAC — the weak, crackable algorithm Kerberoast attackers SPECIFICALLY request because RC4 ticket blobs can be cracked offline against the service account's NTLM hash, whereas AES tickets resist). The six ServiceNames hit: SQLSvc/db01.corp.local (01:09:44), HTTP/app01.corp.local (01:10:02), MSSQLSvc/sql-fin-01.corp.local (01:10:21), HTTP/intranet.corp.local (01:10:38), exchange/mail-corp-01.corp.local (01:10:55), MSSQLSvc/sql-hr-02.corp.local (01:11:13). Six distinct SPNs from a single marketing user in 89 seconds is the unambiguous Invoke-Kerberoast scan signature — a legitimate marketing user has no business requesting tickets for SQL, HR, Exchange, and intranet service accounts.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4769 (data.win.system.eventID=4769) on agent.name=DC-CORP-01, with data.win.eventdata.targetUserName=john.doe + data.win.eventdata.ticketEncryptionType=0x17 + data.win.eventdata.ipAddress=10.30.6.92, six distinct serviceName values within 89 seconds. The univariate Kerberoast signature: single requester + RC4 + multi-SPN burst.", "first_seen": "2028-01-12T01:09:44+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Collection / Local Stage", "technique": "T1074.001", "what_happened": "At 01:12:47 CET, Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) on WS-MARKETING-21 logged targetFilename=C:\\Users\\john.doe\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\kerberoast.txt under user=CORP\\john.doe. This is the standard Invoke-Kerberoast -OutputFormat Hashcat output file: the six service-ticket hashes serialized in Hashcat $krb5tgs$23$* format (the 23 in the $krb5tgs$23$ prefix is the etype identifier matching the 0x17 RC4-HMAC requested in step 2). The file is the cracking-ready artifact ready for offline brute-force.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate): targetFilename contains 'kerberoast.txt' + path in Temp + user=CORP\\john.doe + agent.name=WS-MARKETING-21, ~94 seconds after the last 4769. The temporal alignment (six tickets harvested → output file written ~1.5 min later) matches the Invoke-Kerberoast workflow.", "first_seen": "2028-01-12T01:12:47+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Exfiltration (C2 outbound)", "technique": "T1041 + T1071.001", "what_happened": "At 01:14:33 CET, Sysmon Event 3 (Network Connection) on WS-MARKETING-21 logged an outbound connection to data.dest_ip=185.220.101.91 on port 443 (HTTPS). The destination IP is a previously-known external attacker C2 (referenced in prior internal cases). Temporally aligned with the kerberoast.txt creation (~106 seconds after file write), this connection is the most plausible exfiltration channel for the harvested hashes — sending krb5tgs$23$ blobs out to attacker infrastructure for offline cracking on dedicated GPU clusters where RC4-HMAC of a typical 12-character service account password can be broken in hours.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3: agent.name=WS-MARKETING-21 + data.dest_ip=185.220.101.91 + dest_port=443 + temporal alignment with the kerberoast.txt file creation. The destination is on the org's internal known-bad TI list from prior cases.", "first_seen": "2028-01-12T01:14:33+0100"}
